# snapshotted

Write the application once in `snapshotted_app`. The browser, web SSR server, and native runners render the same screen and behavior. `snapshotted_backend` implements RPC endpoints; `snapshotted_shared` holds their serializable contracts. The root server imports the app and backend, while application builds never import backend implementation.

```sh
leptoskit build
leptoskit dev
leptoskit build --platform ios
leptoskit build --platform android
leptoskit build --platform macos
leptoskit build --platform windows
leptoskit build --platform linux
```

All six destinations are enabled in `leptoskit.toml`. Each build activates exactly one platform feature in `snapshotted_app`; add optional dependencies to that feature when they support the app's actual compilation target. Native app code compiles to Wasm, so UIKit, JNI, and other OS dependencies belong in the corresponding runner's Cargo manifest or a native plugin.

`src/main.rs` starts the SSR server and serves browser files. The backend's `health` server function answers `GET /api/health`, and `#[api]` publishes it: `leptoskit rpc spec` prints the published API, and `leptoskit rpc generate --target typescript` writes a typed client for it. Shared assets live in `assets/`. `web/index.html` supplies the document for CSR mode (`app.render = "csr"`); SSR uses the shared app's `shell` function.

## File routes

Screens live under `snapshotted_app/src/routes/`. The starter opens on Home, with Kit and Platforms beside it in the tab bar. The `router` plugin regenerates `snapshotted_app/src/routes.rs` on `leptoskit dev` and `leptoskit build` (or run `leptoskit plugin:router routes`). Edit the files under `routes/`; do not edit the generated module by hand.

For a module that is not a route — a reusable component, for example — `leptoskit::platform_module!` still picks a per-platform file (`thing.ios.rs`, `thing.android.rs`, …) beside the shared one. Per-platform *route* files are not supported yet; use `cfg!(feature = "…")` inside a route when one platform must differ.

`ios/`, `android/`, `macos/`, `windows/`, and `linux/` are app-owned platform runners. Install the matching SDK when building a destination. All generated registrants, SDK integration files, and build intermediates stay under `.leptoskit/<platform>/`, which is ignored by Git. Prepare a native target before opening its generated Xcode project or the app-owned `android/` Gradle project.

`leptoskit dev` resolves `monosecret.toml` through Monosecret before starting. Install Monosecret, then store credentials in its keyring or your team's provider. The manifest declares names; keep secret values out of Git. Use `leptoskit dev --no-secrets` when your host supplies the environment. In production, run `monosecret run -- ./target/release/snapshotted`. See [secret management](https://leptoskit.dev/docs/guide/secrets.html).

The starter grants no native plugin capabilities unless the app needs them. Add native plugins to the runner's direct Cargo dependencies and grant them explicitly in `native.plugins`.
